# Rust build output
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Coverage
/coverage/
*.profraw
*.profdata
lcov.info

# Release / packaging output
/dist/
/fuzz/target/
*.sbom.json
*.spdx.json
*.tar.gz
*.tar.xz
*.zip

# Python bytecode. `skills/` is a published surface a user copies into their own agent,
# and a CPython-version-specific .pyc in it is build cruft that is not portable and was
# never ours to ship. One got committed before this rule existed.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp
*~
*.log

# Local agent scratch space. Never commit transcripts or scratch analysis.
/.agent-scratch/
/.agent/bootstrap/
/.agent/bootstrap-state.json
/.agent/bootstrap-history/
/.agents/
/.codex/
/.claude/settings.local.json

# Credential material must never be committed. These patterns are a backstop, not a
# control: the real control is that `jev` never writes credentials to the repository.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
secrets.*
credentials.*
.jev/

# The two patterns above are unanchored, so git applies them at every depth -- and
# `credentials.*` therefore matched `crates/jev-config/src/credentials.rs`, silently
# keeping a core source file out of the repository for four commits. Nothing local
# noticed: the file was on disk, so every build and every test passed. CI, checking out
# what was actually committed, failed with "failed to resolve mod `credentials`".
#
# Re-include source, which is never a secret dump. A negation cannot resurrect a file
# whose parent directory is excluded, so this does not reach into `target/`.
# `scripts/check-tracked-sources.py` fails the build if a source file is ignored again.
!crates/**/*.rs
!fuzz/fuzz_targets/*.rs

# Fuzzing. The working corpus is regenerable and can grow to hundreds of megabytes; a
# crash artifact is a finding to turn into a unit test next to the parser that produced
# it, not a file to commit.
/fuzz/corpus/
/fuzz/artifacts/
/fuzz/coverage/

# Explicitly NOT ignored: `fuzz/seeds/` is the small, hand-written seed corpus that
# `scripts/fuzz-smoke.sh` copies in before every run. It is what makes a short CI run
# start from a real parse, and it is where an input that once found a bug lives.
!/fuzz/seeds/

# Explicitly NOT ignored: proptest regression seeds are checked in so that a failure
# found once is replayed forever.
!**/proptest-regressions/

# Local research corpus of third-party reference repositories and documentation
# snapshots. Untrusted material, never executed, never a dependency, never committed.
# See docs/research/reference-manifest.md for the tracked provenance record.
/references/

# Skill-authoring development toolchain. Third-party agent skills, fetched verbatim at
# pinned commits by `scripts/skill-authoring-setup.sh` from `skill-authoring-lock.json`.
# They are not committed: AGENTS.md §8 keeps third-party source out of this repository,
# and the lockfile plus the script make the set reproducible without it. The tracked
# record is `docs/development/skill-authoring.md`.
/.claude/skills/skill-creator/
/.claude/skills/writing-skills/
/.claude/skills/test-driven-development/
/.claude/skills/verification-before-completion/
/.claude/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents/

# Local agent-ready-repo skill.
/.claude/skills/agent-ready-repo/

# Staging tree that `scripts/skill-eval.sh` builds so `claude plugin eval` sees the
# shipped skills as a plugin. Regenerated on every run; never edit it.
/target/skill-evals/

# Skill eval results: HTML reports and per-run JSON. Large, timestamped, and a record of
# one run against one model version rather than a lasting fact about the skill.
/evals/**/results/
